.TH PUT 1 2026-10-31 "put 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
put, put.raw, put.lines, put.nul, put.peek, put.quote, put.json, put.help \- print arguments exactly as given
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B put
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B put.\c
.I mode
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B put
prints its arguments literally.
It has no options and interprets no escape sequences, so
.B put
"$x" prints the value of
.I x
whatever it contains, including
.BR \-n ,
.B \-\-
or backslashes.
.PP
One binary is installed under several names.
The name it is run as selects the mode:
.TP
.B put
Arguments joined by single spaces, then a newline.
With no arguments, prints a newline.
.TP
.B put.raw
Arguments joined by single spaces, with no trailing newline.
.TP
.B put.lines
Each argument followed by a newline.
.TP
.B put.nul
Each argument followed by a NUL byte, for
.BR "xargs \-0" .
.TP
.B put.peek
A numbered line per argument showing what it really contains.
Leading and trailing spaces are shown as \(pc, an empty argument as
\(es empty, control characters as escapes such as \er and \ex07,
invisible Unicode characters (no-break and zero-width spaces, byte order
marks, bidirectional controls, tag characters) as \eu{a0}, and invalid
UTF-8 as \exff.
On a terminal, escapes are colored; otherwise literal backslashes are
doubled and a literal \(pc or \(es is escaped, so every output has one
reading.
.B put.peek
never writes a control byte taken from its input.
.TP
.B put.quote
The arguments as shell input that recreates them, for bash and zsh.
Uses bare words where safe, single quotes for printable text, and
$'...' with \exHH escapes for control bytes, invalid UTF-8 and invisible
characters.
.TP
.B put.json
The arguments as a JSON array of strings.
.TP
.B put.help
A summary of the modes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Writing to standard output failed.
.TP
.B 2
Run under a name that is not
.B put
or a known
.BI put. mode\fR.
.TP
.B 3
.B put.json
was given an argument that is not valid UTF-8.
Nothing is written.
.PP
If the reader of standard output exits early,
.B put
is ended by SIGPIPE, like other Unix tools.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set and not empty,
.B put.peek
does not use color.
No other mode reads the environment.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ x=\-n; put "$x"
\-n
$ put.peek "a " $'b\er' ""
3 args
0  a\(pc
1  b\er
2  \(es empty
$ put.quote it\e's $'tab\ethere'
\(aqit\(aq\e\(aq\(aqs\(aq $\(aqtab\ethere\(aq
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR echo (1),
.BR printf (1),
.BR xargs (1)
